body:
I think your concerned is centered around people trying to use import() for eval. In the browser you can do module eval using inline scripts. This same source code will be executed twice:

``` js
function evalModuleCode(txt) {
  const script = document.createElement('script');
  script.type = 'module';
  script.textContent = txt;
  document.documentElement.appendChild(script);
}

const code = 'document.getElementById("username").value = "foo"';
evalModuleCode(code);
evalModuleCode(code);
```

It would be nice if module eval was defined in JavaScript. I don't know if the Realm idea is still being kicked around, if so that might be a good place.

There's a note in [module identifier resolution](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#integration-with-the-javascript-module-system) that leaves open the possibility in the future to use bare import specifiers. If/when that were to happen I can see a custom loader that allows you to alias a bare specifier to some url. In that case data/blob urls would be useful for producing dynamic modules. Something like:

**index.html**

``` html
<script type="module">
  const url = `data:text/javascript;charset=utf-8,export default ${Math.random()};`;
  customLoader.alias.randomNumber = url;

  import(url).then(_ => import('/main.js'));
</script>
```

**main.js**

``` js
import num from 'randomNumber';
```
